Fitting and conditioning do not act independently: parameters fitted on
measured auxiliary responses help recall with measured conditioning values
but reduce it with permuted values. At a fixed fitted GP and shared
observations, measured versus permuted conditioning selects different
compounds without changing predictive variance. Separately, full pooling
responds strongly to the historical sampling rule. These comparisons
locate several sources of apparent transfer gains that final recall would
otherwise combine.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item We cross auxiliary responses used for GP fitting with responses used
for posterior conditioning, holding historical locations, model parameters,
and normalization fixed during matched-state comparisons.
\item On six protein-family panels, we find a fitting--conditioning
interaction in sequential recall and response-dependent acquisition choices
at equal predictive variance in shared-state replays.
\item We evaluate alternative explanations with uncertainty-rescaled
acquisition and historical-sampling controls, including a response-free
history-avoidance policy for full pooling.
\end{enumerate}

